# Problem #1— Find Duplicate Test IDs

## Problem

Given an array of test IDs, find all IDs that appear more than once.

Each duplicate ID should appear **only once** in the result.

### Examples

```text
["TC001", "TC002", "TC003", "TC002", "TC004", "TC001"]
→ ["TC001", "TC002"]

["TC001", "TC002", "TC003"]
→ []

["A", "B", "A", "C", "B", "A"]
→ ["A", "B"]
```

## Initial Approach

The first solution used nested loops:

* Iterate through each test ID.
* Compare it with previous IDs to determine whether it was already seen.
* When a duplicate was found, search the results array to prevent adding the same duplicate more than once.

This approach was correct but involved repeated searching.

**Time Complexity:** O(n³) worst-case upper bound
**Space Complexity:** O(n)

## Optimization

The key question was:

> **"Have I seen this test ID before?"**

A `Set` is a better fit for this type of lookup.

The optimized approach uses:

* One `Set` to track IDs seen for the first time.
* One `Set` to store duplicates.
* A single pass through the input.

Because the duplicate collection is also a `Set`, repeated duplicates are automatically kept unique.

**Time Complexity:** O(n) average
**Space Complexity:** O(n)

## Edge Cases

* Empty array
* Single element
* No duplicates
* One duplicate appearing multiple times
* Multiple different duplicates

## What I Learned

* How to recognize a **membership-check problem**.
* When to use a `Set` instead of repeatedly searching an array.
* How the right data structure can eliminate nested loops.
* Why optimization should come **after understanding the brute-force solution**.
* A `Set` can also be useful for guaranteeing uniqueness in the output.

## SDET Relevance

Duplicate detection is common in SDET work, such as validating:

* Test case IDs
* API response records
* User IDs
* Database results
* Test data
* Log/event identifiers

The same `Set` pattern can be used to efficiently detect duplicate values in large datasets.
